<h2>Design mistakes that kill conversions</h2>

<p>Most landing pages do not fail because the offer is bad. They fail because the design gets in the way. Here are the most common problems.</p>

<p><strong>Too many choices.</strong> Navigation menus, footer links, sidebar content, multiple offers on one page. Every additional option is a potential exit point. Strip everything out that does not directly support the single action you want visitors to take. Remove your main site navigation. Remove the footer links. Keep it focused.</p>

<p><strong>Slow load times.</strong> If your page takes more than three seconds to load, you have already lost a significant chunk of your visitors. Compress your images. Minimise unnecessary scripts. Test your page speed regularly. A fast, simple page will outperform a beautiful, slow one every time.</p>

<p><strong>Weak or missing headlines.</strong> If a visitor cannot understand what you are offering and why it matters within five seconds of landing on your page, the design has failed. Your headline and subheading should do all the heavy lifting. Do not rely on people reading three paragraphs of body text to understand your value proposition.</p>

<p><strong>Stock photos that feel generic.</strong> A stock photo of two people shaking hands in front of a whiteboard tells your visitor nothing. If you can, use real photos of your work, your team, or your results. Authenticity builds trust far more effectively than polished but impersonal imagery.</p>

<h2>Mobile optimisation is not optional</h2>

<p>More than half of your landing page traffic is likely coming from mobile devices. If you are running ads on social media, that number could be 80% or higher. Your page needs to work perfectly on a phone.</p>

<p>That means large, tappable buttons. Text that is readable without zooming. Forms that are easy to fill out with thumbs. No horizontal scrolling. No elements that overlap or break on smaller screens.</p>

<p>Test your landing page on your own phone before you launch it. Better yet, test it on a few different devices. What looks perfect on a desktop can be unusable on a mobile screen, and you will not know unless you check.</p>

<blockquote><p>If your landing page does not work on a phone, it does not work. Full stop.</p></blockquote>

<div class="divider">...</div>

<h2>A/B testing without overcomplicating it</h2>

<p>A/B testing means creating two versions of your page with one element changed, then sending traffic to both and seeing which performs better. It is the fastest way to improve your conversion rate over time.</p>

<p>Start simple. Test your headline first, because it has the biggest impact on whether people stay or leave. Run version A with one headline and version B with another. Send equal traffic to both. After a few hundred visitors to each, look at which one converted more people.</p>

<p>Then test your CTA button. The colour, the text, the placement. Then your form length. Then your social proof positioning. One change at a time so you know exactly what made the difference.</p>

<p>You do not need sophisticated testing software for this. Many landing page builders include basic A/B testing tools. Even manually swapping your page content every few days and comparing results is better than never testing at all.</p>

<h2>Putting it together</h2>

<p>A landing page that converts is not about clever design tricks or secret formulas. It is about clarity. Make it obvious what you are offering, why it matters, and what the visitor should do next. Remove everything that distracts from that single goal.</p>

<p>Build one landing page. Keep it simple. Send some traffic to it. Measure what happens. Improve based on what you learn. That cycle, repeated consistently, is how small businesses <a href="/blog/sales-funnel-small-business">build a sales funnel</a> that reliably turns visitors into customers.</p>

